Hi Julien,

Thanks for the question. Here are some thoughts on each of your points:
The offset should be supplied separately in the "data" argument as opposed to including in the formula. When you create the data list for "data", the offset should be specified as "offset" as another component of the list. It can either be a single value, a vector with a different offset value for each site, or a matrix with rows corresponding to sites and columns corresponding to visits if the offset varies by both site and visits. If "offset" is included in the "data" list, then it will automatically be incorporated into the model, and you don't need to specify anything in the model formulas.
No, any tests for multicolinearity have to be done manually with any spAbundance/spOccupancy functions.
No, there is no dredge-like function for doing model selection with spAbundance/spOccupancy. Model selection will need to be done manually.
It's totally fine to include a non-random categorical variable in the model if that is what you think makes the most sense. Of course, if there are a lot of levels to that categorical variable, it will be harder to estimate than a random effect. But, if there are only a few (e.g., 2-4) levels, a non-random factor makes more sense.
Hope that helps,

Jeff

